From 1bdca11db5d236f5ea5789b77b1504729fe97f86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 09:58:34 +0000
Subject: software_isp: Apply the tone curve from a lookup table

The software ISP's output transfer function was fixed in the debayers:
a contrast S-curve then x^gamma, computed from two parameters, per pixel
with pow() in the GPU shaders and into a table on the CPU. That leaves
no way to use a tuned tone curve, which is what hardware ISPs and their
tuning data use (and what dynamic range compression needs next).

Pass the debayers a 256-entry tone lookup table in DebayerParams instead
of gamma and contrastExp. The IPA's Adjust algorithm builds it: from the
contrast S-curve and the gamma encoding as before, so output is
unchanged by default, or from a "toneCurve" in the tuning file (a libipa
Pwl of x, y pairs in [0, 1]) unless the application sets Gamma. The CPU
debayer resamples the table into its gamma table; the GPU debayers
sample it as a 256x1 texture with linear filtering, replacing the pow()
calls with three texture lookups per pixel.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
